  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Constructing the Backend**\n\nI\'ve successfully created the Firebase helper module. My focus has shifted to the backend server. I\'m building `/server.ts` to integrate Express and Vite, and importantly, the `/api/analyze-food` endpoint. Now, let\'s... wait...\n\n\n**Investigating Gemini Integration**\n\nI\'m now diving into the Gemini API integration. I need to figure out how to best leverage `@google/genai` on the server-side, with attention to its initialization and model selection. Specifically, I need to account for how this will perform image analysis, even though the instructions mention \'gemini-3.5-flash\' for basic text tasks. I\'m exploring the available models within the skill to find the best match for extracting nutrition and ingredient details from uploaded food images.\n\n\n**Confirming the Model**\n\nI\'ve confirmed that `gemini-3.5-flash` is ideal for multimodal input. It natively supports image analysis without paid flows, so that\'s good. I\'m focusing on the code pattern for multimodal image analysis now, specifically how to structure the image and text parts within the `generateContent` request. I\'m double-checking the supported mime types to make sure there are no issues.\n\n\n**Defining the Output Format**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on defining the output format. I realized Gemini\'s structured output via `responseSchema` is the way to go for guaranteed JSON. I\'m excited about this because it promises a consistent format. I\'m currently figuring out which properties to specify in the schema, and which Types to use within them. This will allow the model to deliver data in a defined format. This is good!\n\n\n**Refining the Response Schema**\n\nI\'ve finalized the response schema using `responseSchema` for Gemini. It\'s clean and efficient! I\'ve defined properties for food name, calories, protein, carbs, fat, and ingredients. I\'ll make sure the response MIME type is JSON. I\'m now exploring another API endpoint for text-based input to enhance the applet\'s robustness. This will be an additional `/api/gemini/analyze-text` route.\n\n\n**Expanding API Endpoints**\n\nNow, I\'m extending the API. I\'m adding a `/api/analyze-text` endpoint for text analysis, mirroring the structure of `/api/analyze-food`. Both will elegantly utilize the existing `responseSchema` for consistent output, which I\'m very happy about. This is very good. I\'m checking the full-stack app guideline for the `server.ts` setup, and I\'m very happy with how it\'s shaping up. I now need static routing, Vite middleware, the JSON parser, and the correct listening port.\n\n\n**Implementing Server Static Routing**\n\nI\'m now integrating static routing and Vite middleware into the `server.ts` file, ensuring smooth operation whether in development or production. I\'m focusing on static file serving and correct SPA handling. I\'m also implementing a fallback for handling undefined API keys gracefully to prevent crashes. The structure is coming together!\n\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will create the `/server.ts` full-stack server. It handles JSON body uploads up to 10MB (important for food pictures), initializes the Gemini API client lazily to avoid boot failure, configures structured JSON schema extraction via `gemini-3.5-flash`, and hooks Vite.js development middleware."
