    modified_response: "I have successfully diagnosed and resolved the permission issues occurring during backend operations in your application.\n\n### Root Cause\nThe Firestore security rules for the profile document (`/users/{userId}`) strictly validated that exactly 10 fields were stored on the **User** entity. However, the onboarding flow in the application also collects and saves the user\'s `gender` profile field, making the actual payload contain 11 fields. This discrepancy between the expected 10 fields and the actual 11 fields caused Firestore\'s rule validator to reject the write request with a \"Missing or insufficient permissions\" error.\n\n### Changes Made\n1. **Blueprints Synchronized**: Updated `firebase-blueprint.json` to explicitly document the `gender` field (defined as an enum supporting `\'male\' | \'female\' | \'other\'`) under the `User` entity definition.\n2. **Security Rules Upgraded**: Modified the `isValidUser` helper in `firestore.rules` to:\n   - Accept the updated key list with size `11`.\n   - Add a type and value guard to confirm `gender` is a string matching either `\'male\'`, `\'female\'`, or `\'other\'`.\n3. **Deployment**: Successfully compiled the application, ran compliance checks, and deployed the hardened, verified rule configuration live to your Firebase project.\n\n> **Note on Security Rules:** While the updated ruleset is robust, tightly matches your app\'s types, and prevents unauthorized requests, it is always recommended to review, test, and continuously monitor your Firestore security rules as your application grows or handles sensitive user data."
