    thinking: "**Analyzing Security Rules**\n\nOkay, I\'m examining the `firestore.rules` file now. I\'ve found some interesting constraints on data size and the `imageUrl` field. Specifically, I\'m looking at lines 48 and 58, which enforce size limits and an optional string validation for `imageUrl`. I\'m starting to think about edge cases.\n\n\n**Implementing Data Sanitization**\n\nI\'ve decided to implement a `sanitizeDbData` function in `/src/lib/firebase.ts` to handle `undefined` values, preventing the Firestore error. This helper will recursively filter out `undefined` fields from payloads, as a safe default for any `setDoc` operation. I\'m now writing and integrating this function into `saveUserProfile` and `addFoodLog` to ensure data integrity. I\'m also double-checking for other potential `undefined` fields.\n\n\n**Refining Data Operations**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the `saveUserProfile` and `addFoodLog` functions. I\'ve been reviewing the data operations, focusing on the potential for `undefined` values to cause issues with Firestore. I\'m building out a plan to refine the `sanitizeDbData` function\'s integration, specifically targeting its application to prevent Firestore errors. I\'m focusing on ensuring data integrity when writing.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will edit `/src/lib/firebase.ts` to add a recursive `sanitizeDbData` helper function and wrap Firestore updates in it. This will automatically strip out any fields with `undefined` values (such as an optional `imageUrl`, `ingredients`, or user profile fields) from being sent in the Firestore payload, preventing the client SDK from throwing \"Unsupported field value: undefined\" errors."
