  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Designing Confirmation UI**\n\nI\'m currently focused on designing an inline confirmation UI, specifically for scenarios involving `deletingLogId`. The core idea revolves around rendering two buttons when `deletingLogId` matches a `log.id`. I\'m considering the button design: a Confirm button that could be green or red depending on the theme, perhaps light shades.\n\n\n**Refining Button Designs**\n\nI\'ve been refining the button designs for the inline confirmation. I\'m leaning towards a simpler approach: a green \"Check\" icon for confirmation (e.g., `bg-emerald-500`) and a grey \"X\" icon for cancellation (e.g., `bg-slate-100`). I\'m also confirming that using a small, inline container is a clean and visually effective solution. I\'m previewing how the UI will look with these design choices.\n\n\n**Implementing Delete Confirmation**\n\nI\'ve implemented the delete confirmation flow, focusing on immediate feedback. The UI now displays a \"Delete?\" label next to two interactive icons: a red \"X\" for confirmation and a grey one for cancellation. The \"X\" performs the delete. Clicking either will stop event propagation. I\'m satisfied with the immediate effect.\n\n\n**Adjusting Cancellation Logic**\n\nI\'m now adjusting the cancellation logic to enhance user experience. I\'ve updated the cancellation button so it sets `deletingLogId` to `null` to ensure the delete confirmation dialog disappears, with `e.stopPropagation()` for event control. The UI feels cleaner and more responsive with this refinement. I have a good sense for how the new UI renders.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 7
      nanos: 932859649
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
