  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Considering Image Compression**\n\nI\'m now exploring image compression within `src/lib/firebase.ts` before calls to `addFoodLog`. The challenge is handling potentially large image files that currently trigger size limit errors during the `addFoodLog` call. My thinking centers on integrating compression directly into the data flow to ensure smooth operations. I\'m focusing on the `src` directory to facilitate implementation.\n\n\n**Revising Image Processing**\n\nI\'m now realizing that `captureFrame` and `processSelectedFile` handle images of varying origins and resolutions, potentially leading to large base64 strings. I\'m starting to think about resizing the images before base64 encoding, especially since the image analysis component doesn\'t need full resolution. I\'m reconsidering the overall workflow to incorporate image resizing, focusing on optimizing image size before potentially sending it to firebase.\n\n\n**Analyzing Image Storage**\n\nI\'ve realized that food contour, texture, and ingredient identification don\'t need high-resolution images. Resizing images to 300px, 400px, or even keeping the file size below ~50KB is more than sufficient. This should keep Firestore documents well under the 1MB limit. I am now looking at `src/components/MealLogger.tsx`, specifically `handleConfirmAndLog` to see where `selectedImageBase64` is assigned and stored, to apply these size restrictions. My focus is on ensuring `imageUrl: selectedImageBase64 || undefined` doesn\'t include overly large files.\n\n\n**Investigating Image Usage**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on how `selectedImageBase64` is used. I\'ve confirmed it\'s rendered directly in the log list using an `<img>` tag. My concern is large base64 strings causing Firestore write failures, so I\'m thinking of building a utility to resize/compress images. I\'m considering compressing when the user uploads/captures images or right before saving to Firestore. I\'m also questioning the size of `selectedImageBase64` sent to `/api/analyze-food`.\n\n\n**Implementing Image Compression**\n\nI\'ve realized immediate image compression upon selection/capture is crucial. This drastically reduces `/api/analyze-food` payload size, upload time, Firestore storage, and display load. A client-side utility in `MealLogger.tsx` will resize images using a `<canvas>` element, targeting a maximum dimension of 400px and JPEG format with 0.7 quality to optimize compression, ensuring efficient storage and performance.\n\n\n**Confirming File Size**\n\nI\'m now confirming my earlier calculations: a 400x300 JPEG encoded with 0.7 quality is indeed around 15KB-25KB, well within the 1MB Firestore limit. I\'ve realized these are perfect thumbnail sizes, and I don\'t need the original. Now I\'m focused on a helper function, possibly inside `MealLogger.tsx`, to compress images.\n\n\n**Refining Image Compression**\n\nI\'ve been working on a utility to resize images, and I now believe the code addresses the need for efficient image handling. I\'m focusing on ensuring it\'s robust, with fallbacks to avoid failure during execution. I\'ve integrated checks for errors with image loading and canvas context, and I\'ve also incorporated a return to the original base64 if there are any errors. The goal is to provide reliable and efficient image processing.\n\n\n**Tracing Image Placement**\n\nI\'m now tracing the usage of `compressImage` to ensure optimal placement. For camera captures, I\'ll integrate compression within `captureFrame` for immediate downsizing and efficiency. For gallery uploads, I\'m integrating the compression directly into `processSelectedFile` using the helper, ensuring consistent size and format. I think these changes will address the issues with data size and loading.\n\n\n**Adjusting Image Format**\n\nI\'m now thinking I can consistently use \'image/jpeg\' after compression, which simplifies the process and avoids having to detect or maintain multiple MIME types. I will update `processSelectedFile` to automatically set `selectedImageMime` to \'image/jpeg\' when using `compressImage`. I\'m now also considering using the `compressImage` utility in `captureFrame` to normalize all images to this format.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will list the directory contents of the root folder to see if a custom server file (`server.ts` or similar) is present."
