    thinking: "**Optimizing Image Handling**\n\nI\'ve realized the server can handle larger JSON uploads, up to 15MB, but Firestore has a 1MB limit. Thus, I\'m now certain that client-side image optimization is the ideal solution. Reducing resolution and compression will maximize efficiency when uploading images.\n\n\n**Structuring Image Compression**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the implementation of the `compressImage` function within `MealLogger.tsx`. I\'ve decided it belongs at the top, outside the React component. I\'m building out the function\'s structure, including default values for `maxWidth` and `quality`. Currently, I\'m setting up the core image resizing logic using a canvas element to handle the image manipulation.\n\n\n**Refining Compression Details**\n\nI\'ve just added the image resizing and compression logic within the `compressImage` function. I\'m using a canvas element for this, drawing the image to it and then converting it to a data URL using \'image/jpeg\' with a quality of 0.5. I\'m aiming for very small file sizes, between 8KB and 15KB. I\'m confident in the speed of the operation in modern browsers. I\'ve also set the `maxWidth` to 300. I also handle potential errors in the process gracefully, and handle image loading errors.\n\n\n**Integrating Compression Workflow**\n\nI\'m now integrating the image compression into the file processing logic. I\'m modifying `processSelectedFile` in `MealLogger.tsx` to utilize the `compressImage` function. It will now handle image file type checks and will set the compressed base64 string to the state, ensuring that the image size remains under Firestore limits.\n\n\n**Implementing Image Capture**\n\nI\'m now integrating image capture from the video feed. I\'m focusing on the `captureFrame` function to grab a snapshot. I\'m checking for video and canvas refs, then drawing the current video frame onto the canvas. I\'m setting the canvas dimensions to match the video. I\'m preparing to convert the canvas content to a base64 string, allowing image uploads from the camera.\n\n\n**Enhancing Capture Process**\n\nI\'m now refining the `captureFrame` function to incorporate image compression immediately after capturing. I\'m taking the raw base64 data and invoking `compressImage` with a `maxWidth` of 300 and a compression quality of 0.5. I\'m updating the `setSelectedImageBase64` state with the compressed base64 string, so the captured image is immediately efficient. I\'m also handling potential errors during the compression phase and I am now stopping the viewfinder streams and transitioning to an upload confirmation mode after compression.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will edit `/src/components/MealLogger.tsx` to add the `compressImage` helper function, and use it inside `captureFrame` and `processSelectedFile` to resize and compress any user images down to a super-lightweight format (maximum 300px dimension and 0.5 quality JPEG, ~10-15KB). This guarantees that none of the food log entries can ever exceed the 1MB Firestore limit."
